                <pb facs="0456" n="449" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg449"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/9517: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-08-19T23:00:00+09:00">August 19, 1937—11
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received August 19—3:25 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>283. Embassy’s 279, August 18, 6 p.m.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>The Vice Minister for Foreign Affairs today informed the British Chargé
                        d’Affaires that Japan could not accept the British proposal for
                        Chinese-Japanese withdrawal of forces in and around Shanghai on condition
                        that foreign powers would assume responsibility for the safety of Japanese
                        nationals in the International Settlement and on the extra-Settlement roads
                        because (1) foreign forces would not be adequate; (2) it is Japan’s duty to
                        protect her own nationals, and (3) the Chinese are entirely to blame for the
                        present situation.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Dodds however obtained assurances that this refusal was only <hi
                            rendition="#italic">pro tern</hi> and might be reconsidered.</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>Dodds believes that the Japanese realize that they are “in a jam” in
                        Shanghai and that reconsideration of their refusal to accept the British
                        proposal is not unlikely.</item>
                    <label>4.</label>
                    <item>The French Ambassador has informed the Foreign Office that France will
                        undertake to protect Japanese in the French concession provided that the
                        powers undertake the protection of Japanese in the International
                        Settlement.</item>
                </list>
                <p>Repeated to Nanking.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
